s)", "Chronic alcohol abuse (5 drinks/day) and NSAID use (ibuprofen) as risk factors for gastrointestinal bleeding"], "excluded_candidates": ["Anxiety exacerbation: less likely due to objective hemodynamic instability and localized abdominal findings", "Alcohol-related liver disease: possible but less immediately explanatory for acute hemodynamic compromise", "Ovarian pathology (e.g., ruptured cyst): considered but requires β-hCG results to confirm"], "alarm_features": ["Hypotension with tachycardia (shock criteria)", "Severe localized abdominal pain with systemic instability", "Recent unexplained weight loss"]}, "branches": {"B1": {"id": "B1", "label": "Gynecologic Pelvic Emergency", "parent": "ROOT", "level": 1, "status": "live", "prior": 0.25, "posterior": 0.3947368421052632, "danger": 0.9, "actionability": 0.0, "explanatory_coverage": 0.0, "expand_score": 0.5, "evidence_for": ["Chronic alcohol and NSAID use moderately support acute pancreatitis as the pancreatic emergency, aligning with risk factors but lacking confirmatory symptoms l…"], "evidence_against": ["Lack of adnexal tenderness weakly contradicts ovarian torsion as the primary gynecologic pelvic emergency, but β-hCG testing is pending to clarify pregnancy-re…"], "unresolved_questions": [], "children": [], "closure_reason": "", "turn_cost_to_refine": 1.0, "diagnosis_commitment_gain": 0.8, "interrupt_relevance": 0.9, "level_role": "family", "classification_axis": "anatomy", "representative_diseases": []}, "B2": {"id": "B2", "label": "Hepatic/Hepatobiliary Vascular Emergency", "level": 1, "status": "parked", "posterior": 0.211, "danger": 0.8, "parent": "ROOT", "closure_reason": "", "evidence_against": ["The subacute-to-acute pain pattern during exercise is neutral for hepatobiliary vascular emergencies, as acute exacerbations can occur in chronic conditions li…"]}, "B3": {"id": "B3", "label": "Biliary Obstruction", "level": 1, "status": "parked", "posterior": 0.132, "danger": 0.6, "parent": "ROOT", "closure_reason": "", "evidence_against": ["Lack of jaundice or fever moderately contradicts biliary obstruction as the cause, but recent weight loss and diffuse tenderness maintain some plausibility."]}, "B4": {"id": "B4", "label": "Pancreatic Emergency", "parent": "ROOT", "level": 1, "status": "live", "prior": 0.3, "posterior": 0.2631578947368421, "danger": 0.75, "actionability": 0.0, "explanatory_coverage": 0.0, "expand_score": 0.5, "evidence_for": ["Chronic alcohol and NSAID use moderately support acute pancreatitis as the pancreatic emergency, aligning with risk factors but lacking confirmatory symptoms l…"], "evidence_against": ["Absence of typical pancreatitis symptoms (nausea, epigastric pain) moderately contradicts the pancreatic emergency branch, though alcohol/NSAID risk factors re…"], "unresolved_questions": [], "children": [], "closure_reason": "", "turn_cost_to_refine": 1.0, "diagnosis_commitment_gain": 0.7, "interrupt_relevance": 0.75, "level_role": "family", "classification_axis": "anatomy", "representative_diseases": []}}, "frontier": ["B1", "B4"], "other_mass": 0.0, "pending_results": [], "actions_taken": [{"t": 1, "type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "Does the patient's RUQ pain with diffuse tenderness and recent weight loss suggest a mechanical obstruction from a large stone (as per RAG context)?", "summary": "The combination of RUQ pain and weight loss weakly supports biliary obstruction, but the absence of jaundice or obstructive features weakens this hypothesis."}, {"t": 1, "type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "Does the patient's chronic alcohol use and NSAID (ibuprofen) intake increase the plausibility of acute pancreatitis as the pancreatic emergency?", "summary": "Chronic alcohol and NSAID use moderately support acute pancreatitis as the pancreatic emergency, aligning with risk factors but lacking confirmatory symptoms like epigastric radiation or nausea."}, {"t": 1, "type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "Does the absence of adnexal tendernes