mic instability and RUQ pain are moderately supportive of gynecologic pelvic emergencies but do not decisively confirm this category due to overlapping features with other acute abdominal processes.", "Chronic alcohol use weakly supports hepatobiliary vascular emergencies, but the acute-onset presentation and lack of chronic vascular findings (e.g., ascites, collateral veins) reduce this hypothesis' strength.", "The combination of RUQ pain and weight loss weakly supports biliary obstruction, but the absence of jaundice or obstructive features weakens this hypothesis.", "Chronic alcohol and NSAID use moderately support acute pancreatitis as the pancreatic emergency, aligning with risk factors but lacking confirmatory symptoms like epigastric radiation or nausea."], "evidence_against": ["Lack of adnexal tenderness weakly contradicts ovarian torsion as the primary gynecologic pelvic emergency, but β-hCG testing is pending to clarify pregnancy-related causes."], "unresolved_questions": [], "askable_discriminators": ["History of amenorrhea or pregnancy symptoms", "Presence of adnexal tenderness on exam"], "requestable_discriminators": ["Urinary β-hCG (already ordered)", "Pelvic ultrasound"]}, "target_level": 2}
